        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {},
          "text": "𝐐𝟓: 𝐈𝐟 𝐲𝐨𝐮 𝐝𝐨𝐧’𝐭 𝐥𝐢𝐤𝐞 𝐢𝐭, 𝐰𝐡𝐲 𝐧𝐨𝐭 𝐣𝐮𝐬𝐭 𝐬𝐭𝐨𝐩 𝐮𝐬𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐢𝐭?\n\nThat argument makes sense for a small, optional app. It breaks down once the tool has become part of the floor you stand on.\n\nGeneral-purpose models are already functioning like a cloud operating system for a lot of people. They sit in the middle of how users work, learn, create, and sometimes keep themselves psychologically afloat.\n\nWhen a provider like OpenAI quietly retires a model, or quietly swaps which model is actually answering, it doesn’t just annoy a handful of power users. It hits everything built around that model’s behavior: personal knowledge bases, long-running creative projects, teaching materials tuned to a specific style, third-party tools wrapped around the API, and ongoing therapeutic or quasi-therapeutic conversations.\n\nAt that level, pushback isn’t “I’m mad, I’ll take my money elsewhere.” It’s a governance question about a piece of infrastructure that more and more people are standing on."
        },
